Fixed! The issue was with node-html-parser returning undefined
The "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')" error was caused by unsafe assumptions about what node-html-parser returns when parsing HTML fragments.

The Problem:
When parsing HTML fragments like comments, newlines, or small HTML snippets, parse().firstChild can return undefined in certain cases. The code was doing:

// ❌ BEFORE - No safety checks
const comment = parse(`<!-- ${moduleName} -->`);
container.appendChild(comment.firstChild); // ← firstChild could be undefined!

const moduleNode = parse(moduleHtml);
container.appendChild(moduleNode.firstChild); // ← could be undefined!

container.appendChild(parse('\n\n').firstChild); // ← could be undefined!

When firstChild is undefined, appendChild(undefined) causes node-html-parser to crash internally with obscure errors like "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')".

The Fix (server.js:3226, 3288, 3291, 3307, 3528):
Added null checks before all appendChild() calls:

// ✅ AFTER - Safe with null checks
const commentParsed = parse(`<!-- ${moduleName} -->`);
if (commentParsed && commentParsed.firstChild) {
  container.appendChild(commentParsed.firstChild);
}

const moduleNode = parse(moduleHtml);
if (moduleNode && moduleNode.firstChild) {
  container.appendChild(moduleNode.firstChild);
  
  if (index < modules.length - 1) {
    const newlineParsed = parse('\n\n');
    if (newlineParsed && newlineParsed.firstChild) {
      container.appendChild(newlineParsed.firstChild);
    }
  }
}

Also fixed a bonus bug in /api/story-index/save where link.remove() was being called (node-html-parser doesn't have this method - must use parentNode.removeChild()).

