Fixed! Module Saving Issue Resolved
I found and fixed the critical bug that was preventing modules from being saved to pages.

The Problem
In server.js:3287, when the save endpoint was generating HTML for modules, the template strings included leading whitespace:

moduleHtml = `
      <div
        id="module-${module.id}"
        data-module="${module.moduleType}"
        ...
      ></div>`;

When this HTML was parsed with parse(moduleHtml), the firstChild property returned a TextNode containing the leading whitespace, not the actual <div> element. This meant the null check passed, but nothing was actually appended to the container.

The Solution
I added .trim() to remove leading/trailing whitespace before parsing:

// Trim moduleHtml to avoid firstChild being a text node with whitespace
const moduleNode = parse(moduleHtml.trim());

Now firstChild correctly returns the div element, and modules are properly saved to pages.

